    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll try to stabilize the temp. But what about the flour side of things? I've been mixing in more rye lately because of the enzymatic activity, but it seems to make the acidity spikes even more erratic. How does the specific protein content or the mineral content in the rye affect the microbial balance compared to the AP flour?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rye flour, especially, can be a wild card in levain stability. It's inherently more complex than AP flour, which can sometimes lead to more unpredictable microbial behavior, like those erratic spikes you're seeing. The higher mineral content in rye, and the different enzyme profile, can feed certain types of wild bacteria more aggressively than the yeast alone. To try and stabilize things, you might consider gradually reducing the rye percentage or ensuring you are using a high-quality, freshly milled rye. A very high protein AP flour, on the other hand, provides a more consistent, easily digestible food source for the yeast, which can sometimes lead to a more predictable, slower rise.",
