    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, the rye definitely seems to be the trigger for these sudden shifts. It's like the culture just takes off on its own and then burns itself out. Is there a way to quantify that microbial \"unpredictability\"? Like, is it just a matter of the enzymes working too fast, or is there something else going on in the jar that's causing that sudden loss of control?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantifying that microbial \"unpredictability\" is tricky, but it usually points to an imbalance in the acid production versus the nutrient consumption rate. When the enzymes in the rye are working too fast, they can rapidly break down starches into simple sugars, giving the bacteria a sudden, massive sugar rush to produce acid. This rapid acid spike can then inhibit the very yeast that is supposed to be doing the rising, causing the culture to stall or \"burn out,\" which is a classic sign of over-acidification.\n\nYou could try feeding your levain at a higher ratio—say, 1:3:3 (1 part starter, 3 parts flour, 3 parts water) instead of a smaller feeding—to dilute the existing microbial load and slow down the rate of activity. This gives the microbes time to regulate themselves before they overproduce acid. If the problem persists, you might need to adjust your hydration slightly; sometimes a slightly lower hydration can temper the initial explosive activity.",
      "source": "target",
